In C++, a deadlock occurs when two or more threads wait indefinitely for resources held by each other. With two mutexes, 
a common cause is acquiring the same mutexes in opposite orders.

For a staff-level MAANG interview, understand both how to reproduce the deadlock and why `std::scoped_lock` fixes it.

## 1. Demonstrate a deadlock with two mutexes

Consider two mutexes, `m1` and `m2`, shared by two threads.

- Thread 1 locks `m1` and then tries to lock `m2`.
- Thread 2 locks `m2` and then tries to lock `m1`.

### Deadlock sequence

#### Thread 1

`lock(m1)`

`wait for m2`

#### Thread 2

`lock(m2)`

`wait for m1`

Thread 1 holds m1 while waiting for m2; Thread 2 holds m2 while waiting for m1. Neither can proceed.

### Deadlocking code

```

#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>

std::mutex m1;
std::mutex m2;

void thread1() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock1(m1);
    std::cout << "Thread 1 acquired m1\n";

    // Thread 1 may pause here while holding m1.
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(100));

    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock2(m2);
    std::cout << "Thread 1 acquired m2\n";
}

void thread2() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock1(m2);
    std::cout << "Thread 2 acquired m2\n";

    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(100));

    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock2(m1);
    std::cout << "Thread 2 acquired m1\n";
}

int main() {
    std::thread t1(thread1);
    std::thread t2(thread2);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();
}

```

Note: Add `#include <chrono>` for `std::chrono::milliseconds`.

The sleep makes the problematic interleaving easier to reproduce, but deadlock is not guaranteed on every run. If both threads acquire their first 
mutex before either acquires its second, they can deadlock. Once deadlocked, `join()` also blocks indefinitely.

## 2. Fix the deadlock using `std::scoped_lock`

C++17 introduced `std::scoped_lock`, which can acquire multiple mutexes together using a deadlock-avoidance algorithm.

Instead of acquiring the mutexes one by one in different orders, pass both mutexes to the same `std::scoped_lock`.

```

#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>

std::mutex m1;
std::mutex m2;

void thread1() {
    std::scoped_lock lock(m1, m2);

    std::cout << "Thread 1 acquired both mutexes\n";

    // Safely access shared resources protected by m1 and m2.
}

void thread2() {
    std::scoped_lock lock(m2, m1);

    std::cout << "Thread 2 acquired both mutexes\n";

    // Safely access shared resources protected by m1 and m2.
}

int main() {
    std::thread t1(thread1);
    std::thread t2(thread2);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();

    std::cout << "Both threads completed\n";
}

```

Compile and run:

```
g++ -std=c++17 -pthread deadlock.cpp -o deadlock
./deadlock
```

Both threads can complete, regardless of the order in which they list the mutexes in `std::scoped_lock`.

### Why does this fix work?

1. Deadlock avoidance: `std::scoped_lock` uses a deadlock-avoidance mechanism when acquiring multiple mutexes. If it cannot acquire them all, 
it can release acquired locks and retry rather than indefinitely holding one while waiting for another.
2. RAII: The lock object automatically releases both mutexes when it goes out of scope, including when the function exits through an exception.
3. Order independence: Both threads can request the same mutex set without having to agree on a manual locking order.

## 3. `std::lock_guard` vs. `std::scoped_lock`

| Feature                                 | `std::lock_guard`               | `std::scoped_lock`                |
| --------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Standard                                | C++11                           | C++17                             |
| Single mutex                            | Yes                             | Yes                               |
| Multiple mutexes                        | No, one mutex per guard         | Yes                               |
| Deadlock avoidance for multiple mutexes | Not by itself                   | Yes, when acquiring them together |
| Automatic unlock                        | Yes                             | Yes                               |
| Typical use                             | Simple, single-mutex protection | Multiple-mutex protection         |

An alternative before C++17 is to use `std::lock(m1, m2)` to acquire both mutexes safely and then adopt the locks with `std::lock_guard` or `std::unique_lock`. 
In modern C++, `std::scoped_lock` is generally the simpler option.

## 4. Staff-level interview takeaway

A strong interview answer should mention that deadlock traditionally has four necessary conditions:

- Mutual exclusion: A resource can be held by only one thread at a time.
- Hold and wait: A thread holds one resource while waiting for another.
- No preemption: A resource cannot simply be taken away from its owner.
- Circular wait: Threads form a cycle of dependencies.

`std::scoped_lock` helps prevent deadlock when acquiring multiple mutexes as a group. It does not eliminate every possible deadlock in a program—for example, deadlocks 
involving other locks acquired separately or inconsistent locking across different code paths can still occur.

Interview one-liner: “`std::scoped_lock` provides RAII-based ownership of multiple mutexes and uses deadlock-avoidance logic to acquire them together, eliminating the classic 
opposite-lock-order deadlock.”
